{"records":[{"id":"5z7ppzwpcw","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR/Cas9 screening of the deubiquitinase subfamily identifies USP49 as a protein stabilizer of PAX9 and MSX1 regulating odontogenesis","description":"Deubiquitination of proteins by DUBs has emerged as a key regulator of protein stability and has been shown to be critically involved in signal transduction cascades. However, the function and regulation of individual DUBs are not well understood at the molecular level. Several screening approaches to identify DUBs and their target protein substrates in diverse cellular functions have been reported previously. For example, shRNA/siRNA libraries targeting different DUBs or overexpression of ectopically introduced DUBs have been used to investigate the impact of DUB function on target substrate protein stability. These conventional methods have several limitations, such as the limitation of RNAi or shRNA-based methods to transcripts, and these techniques often result in partial repression or restoration of gene functions in a few generations, with temporary effects.  The results obtained from these methods may not be accurate and might lead to misinterpretations of the data. To overcome this, we developed a loss-of-function study based on individually generated single cell-derived biallelic DUB knockout clones in mammalian cells and provide an alternate and improved model to explore the molecular mechanism of DUBs.\n\nIn this study, we used CRISPR/Cas9 (clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats/CRISPR-associated) mediated DUB knockout screening kit based on genome-scale knockout of the entire set of genes that encode human ubiquitin-specific proteases (USPs), which are the largest subfamily of DUBs (>50 genes), and then we systematically applied the panel to screen DUB candidates that might regulate PAX9 and MSX1 proteins. PAX9 and MSX1 are critical for the transition in odontogenic potential from epithelium to mesenchyme. Although genetic mutations in PAX9 and MSX1 cause tooth agenesis, the regulation of PAX9 and MSX1 protein levels by deubiquitinating enzymes (DUBs) might be a crucial factor during tooth development. Our screening system identified USP49 as a key regulator of PAX9 and MSX1 protein stability. USP49 interacts and deubiquitinates PAX9 and MSX1 protein and upregulates several odontogenic transcription factors in human dental pulp stem cells (hDPSCs). In animal models, USP49 depletion was correlated with downregulation of Pax9 and Msx1 protein expression resulting in several morphological defects in calcified tissues such as reduced dentin growth, reduced enamel space, abnormal enamel formation, and irregular mineralization. Thus, the depletion of USP49 leads to an inadequate transition from bud to cap stage in the early stages of tooth development. This novel finding suggests that the USP49-PAX9-MSX1 axis could serve as a prognostic marker and novel regulatory target in odontogenesis and can serve as a catalyst for bioengineering tooth implant research to improve clinical care.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-05-08T12:30:21.375Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5z7ppzwpcw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5z7ppzwpcw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Suresh","last_name":"Ramakrishna","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Cell Biology","Stem Cell","Animal Development","Tooth Enamel","Tooth Development"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mmnwww6n3s","title":"LZTFL1 Rewires NADPH–Glutathione Metabolism to Amplify Ferroptosis","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent form of regulated cell death, represents a therapeutic vulnerability in apoptosis-resistant cancers. Here, we identify leucine zipper transcription factor-like 1 (LZTFL1) as a key regulator of ferroptosis that rewires glutathione (GSH) metabolism. A genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screen and multi-omics analysis revealed that LZTFL1 promotes oxidation of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD), limiting NADPH production and GSH regeneration. GSH depletion in turn enhanced LZTFL1 translation via an AKT–mTOR–eIF4E pathway, establishing a feed-forward loop that amplifies ferroptosis. In vivo, the LZTFL1–FHOD1–G6PD axis sensitized xenograft and patient-derived xenograft models to ferroptosis, correlating with lipid peroxidation, reduced GSH, and extended survival. Clinically, LZTFL1 expression restored cisplatin efficacy in resistant lung and ovarian cancer cells and predicted better survival outcomes in LUAD patients. Moreover, FDA-approved compounds upregulated LZTFL1 and re-sensitized resistant tumors to cisplatin. These findings define LZTFL1 as both a biomarker and therapeutic target for enhancing ferroptosis-based cancer therapy. The files below contain raw western blot images and flow cytometry data from this study.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-12T06:59:22.445Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mmnwww6n3s","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mmnwww6n3s"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"XIANGFEI","last_name":"XUE","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Flowmetry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y8dndzn3mg","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication","description":"These data are part of the Supplemental Items associated with the manuscript entitled \"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication\". These files include Supplementary Data file 1-4.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-16T10:34:13.579Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y8dndzn3mg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y8dndzn3mg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Limeng","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vg3r5355bj","title":"Code used for \"  An evolutionary model identifies the main selective pressures for the evolution of genome-replication profiles.\" ","description":"Code used for \"  An evolutionary model identifies the main selective pressures for the evolution of genome-replication profiles.\"\n\nAt each cell cycle, a yeast cell replicates its genome by a prescribed but stochastic temporal program encoded by the localization and strength of a set of discrete \"replication origins\" along the genome. This temporal program is of primary importance for cell viability and, consequently, on evolutionary time scale it is subject to selective pressures. By mathematical modelling of replication-origins evolution this study identifies the two main selective pressures responsible for evolutionary appearance and disappearance of replication origins.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-13T21:38:07.95Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vg3r5355bj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vg3r5355bj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"rossana","last_name":"droghetti","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Computer Simulation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bpcpfns6vb","title":"Global genetic interaction network of a human cell maps conserved principles and informs functional interpretation of gene co-essentiality profiles. Billmann et al.","description":"Deciphering how genes interact within human cells is essential for understanding their functional wiring and the development of targeted therapeutic strategies. In this study, we present a genome-scale map of genetic interactions in the human haploid cell line HAP1, based on CRISPR-based perturbation of ~ 4 million gene pairs. The resulting network comprises ~89,000 high-confidence gene-gene interactions, organizing genes into hierarchical modules corresponding to protein complexes and pathways, biological processes, and cellular compartments, mirroring principles observed in yeast and highlighting the functional architecture of a human cell. This large-scale genetic network complements the DepMap gene co-essentiality network by capturing unique functional information, uncovering roles of previously uncharacterized genes, and identifying molecular determinants of cancer cell line-specific genetic dependencies. This study presents a general data-driven strategy analysis for systematically exploring the roles of genes and their functional connections in human cell lines.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-18T10:15:12.084Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bpcpfns6vb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bpcpfns6vb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Maximilian","last_name":"Billmann","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Costanzo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiang","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Arshia Z.","last_name":"Hassan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mahfuzur","last_name":"Rahman","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kevin R.","last_name":"Brown","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Katherine S.","last_name":"Chan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Amy Hin Yan","last_name":"Tong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Carles","last_name":"Pons","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Henry N.","last_name":"Ward","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Catherine","last_name":"Ross","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jolanda","last_name":"van Leeuwen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Aregger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Keith A.","last_name":"Lawson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Amy F.","last_name":"Roth","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nesli E.","last_name":"Sen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Duncan T.","last_name":"Forster","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Guihong","last_name":"Tan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Patricia","last_name":"Mero","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanna N.","last_name":"Masud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yoonkyu","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Magali","last_name":"Aguilera-Uribe","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Matej","last_name":"Usaj","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sylvia M. T.","last_name":"Almeida","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kamaldeep","last_name":"Aulakh","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Urvi","last_name":"Bhojoo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Saba","last_name":"Birkadze","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nathaniel","last_name":"Budijono","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xunhui","last_name":"Cai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joseph J.","last_name":"Caumanns","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jordan J.","last_name":"Chalmers","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Megha","last_name":"Chandrashekhar","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Chang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ryan","last_name":"Climie","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kuheli","last_name":"Dasgupta","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Adrian","last_name":"Drazic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jose I.","last_name":"Echenique","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rafael","last_name":"Gacesa","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Adrian","last_name":"Granda Farias","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andrea","last_name":"Habsid","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ira","last_name":"Horecka","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kristin","last_name":"Kantautas","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fenghu","last_name":"Ji","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dae-Kyum","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Seon Yong","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wendy","last_name":"Liang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hyobin Julianne","last_name":"Lim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kevin","last_name":"Lin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xueyibing","last_name":"Lu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Maier","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Babak","last_name":"Nami","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Allison","last_name":"Nixon","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nicholas","last_name":"Mikolajewicz","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Milad","last_name":"Mokhtaridoost","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lyudmila","last_name":"Nedyalkova","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thomas","last_name":"Rohde","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Maria","last_name":"Sartori Rodrigues","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Martin","last_name":"Soste","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Eric","last_name":"Schultz","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wen","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ashwin","last_name":"Seetharaman","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ermira","last_name":"Shuteriqi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Olga","last_name":"Sizova","orcid":null},{"first_name":"David","last_name":"Thomson Taylor","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Maria","last_name":"Tereshchenko","orcid":null},{"first_name":"David","last_name":"Tieu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jacob","last_name":"Turowec","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tajinder","last_name":"Ubhi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sylvia","last_name":"Varland","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kyle E.","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zi Yang","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jiarun","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null},{"first_name":"YUXI","last_name":"XIAO","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Philipp G.","last_name":"Maass","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bruno","last_name":"Reversade","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Grant W.","last_name":"Brown","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bejamin F.","last_name":"Cravatt","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Scott","last_name":"Dixon","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Haley D. M.","last_name":"Wyatt","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hannes L.","last_name":"Röst","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Frederick P.","last_name":"Roth","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tian","last_name":"Xia","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gary D.","last_name":"Bader","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Robbie","last_name":"Loewith","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nicholas G.","last_name":"Davis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Brenda","last_name":"Andrews","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chad","last_name":"Myers","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jason","last_name":"Moffat","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Charles","last_name":"Boone","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":"MOP_142735"},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","grant_id":"MCB1818293"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h531d29","name":"Natural Sciences and Engineering Research Council of Canada","grant_id":"RGPIN-2020-04180"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":"PJT 173542"},{"id":"https://ror.org/000az4664","name":"Canada Foundation for Innovation","grant_id":"39977"},{"id":"https://ror.org/051639p85","name":"Ontario Research Foundation","grant_id":"RE09-011"},{"id":"https://ror.org/051639p85","name":"Ontario Research Foundation","grant_id":"RE011-006"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":"PJT-180285"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":"PJT-GMX-463531"},{"id":null,"name":"McLauglin Centre Accelerator","grant_id":"MC-2024-08-02"},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","grant_id":"DFG Bi 2086/1-1"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"R01HG005084"},{"id":null,"name":"McLauglin Centre Accelerator","grant_id":"MC-2022-02"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"R01HG00583"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"T32GM008347"},{"id":null,"name":"NIH/NCI Ruth L. Kirschstein National Research F30 Award","grant_id":"5F30CA257227"}],"categories":["Genetics","Biological Network"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rdhxwbhmvn","title":"Unified cancer and non-cancer transcriptomic data; cancer signatures identified by the TAVNIT software","description":"This dataset contains unified cancer and non-cancer transcriptomic data. Here are data for the entire transcriptome and surfaceome (Supp 1). The data are applicable as input for the TAVNIT software. TAVNIT is a novel tool for cancer subtyping and subsequent extraction of druggable cancer signatures.\nAnother part of the deposition is the output of TAVNIT executions – cell-surface cancer signatures. The cancer signature represents a record outlining transcription values of some number of genes that are largely sufficient to distinguish cancer samples from non-cancer samples in a given dataset. The deposited signatures are cell-surface ones. Their respective proteins can be potential targets for CAR-T cells. The signatures have been extracted for all cancer samples (Supp 2) and for prostate adenocarcinoma samples (Supp 3).","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-11-15T11:04:07.875Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rdhxwbhmvn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rdhxwbhmvn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Dmitri","last_name":"Finaev","orcid":"0000-0003-3470-8501"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Transcriptomics","Human"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nnxh5d35nd","title":"Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screen identified KLF11 as a druggable suppressor for sarcoma cancer stem cells","description":"The original, unprocessed data alongside the paper","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-05-31T01:39:45.722Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nnxh5d35nd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nnxh5d35nd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Le","last_name":"Qu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9x47wzy8x7","title":"A study of \"Genome-wide CRISPR screens reveal host factors critical for SARS-CoV-2 infection\" by Wei et al. Cell 2020","description":"Identification of host genes essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection may reveal novel therapeutic targets and inform our understanding of COVID-19 pathogenesis. Here, we performed genome-wide CRISPR screens in Vero-E6 cells with SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV, bat coronavirus HKU5 expressing the SARS-CoV-1 spike, and VSV expressing the SARS-CoV-2 spike. Together this identifies potential therapeutic targets for SARS-CoV-2 and reveals SARS-lineage specific and pan-coronavirus host factors that regulate susceptibility to highly pathogenic coronaviruses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-15T21:29:14.06Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9x47wzy8x7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9x47wzy8x7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Craig","last_name":"Wilen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR","Middle East Respiratory Syndrome-Related Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"x29rjbpk8r","title":"Raw Data for \"A chemical screen identifies PRMT5 as a therapeutic vulnerability for paclitaxel-resistant triple-negative breast cancer\"","description":"This file contains the following raw data for \"A chemical screen identifies PRMT5 as a therapeutic vulnerability for paclitaxel-resistant triple-negative breast cancer\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-06-24T09:06:29.878Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/x29rjbpk8r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/x29rjbpk8r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Juan","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sheyu","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biologicals"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cx428gxkf7","title":"Increased Risk of Subsequent Primary Malignancies in Melanoma versus Non-Melanoma Skin Cancer Patients ","description":"This population-based cohort study performed utilizing the TriNetX database compares the risk of subsequent primary malignancies in patients diagnosed with melanoma or non-melanoma skin cancer, including basal cell carcinomas and squamous cell carcinomas. A case cohort was identified using ICD-10-CM codes for melanoma, basal cell carcinoma, and squamous cell carcinoma, as well as 11 of the most common primary malignancies as defined by the American Cancer Society. These included breast, prostate, lung, colorectal, bladder, non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma, kidney, uterine, leukemia, pancreas, and thyroid cancer. \n\nA total of 130,526 melanoma patients and 130,526 non-melanoma skin cancer patients were identified after propensity score matching for demographics. The adjusted risk ratios revealed that melanoma patients had a significantly increased risk of developing breast, prostate, lung, kidney, pancreatic, and thyroid cancer compared to NMSC patients. No significant differences were found in the risk of developing colorectal cancer, prostate cancer, bladder cancer, non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma, or leukemia between the two groups.\n\nOverall, this TriNetX database study underscores the importance of tailored cancer surveillance, particularly for breast, prostate, lung, kidney, pancreatic, and thyroid cancer in melanoma survivors. Future research should focus on exploring the mechanisms behind the differential cancer risks observed in this study and refining screening strategies for higher-risk populations.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-11-18T09:15:18.894Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cx428gxkf7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cx428gxkf7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Donna","last_name":"Pham","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Canine Dermatology","Derivatization"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":140642,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":177,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":140642,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":139926,"restricted":67,"embargoed":649},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":3475},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2254},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":701},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":463},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":428},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":420},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":405},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":394},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":339},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":268}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":138056},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1860},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":180},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":135},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":77},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":65},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":55},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":39},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":28}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":2966},{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":2307},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":490},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":407},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":371}]}}